[=#keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg]
= keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg =

Oracle's //keep (dense_rank first|last order by ...)// clause on //max()// and //min()// picks the aggregated column's value from whichever row sorts first (or last) within each group.  !PostgreSQL has no //keep()// syntax, and a window function can't substitute for it in a grouped aggregate query, so the '''keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg''' module rewrites the call as an ordered //array_agg()// and takes its first element.

A query like:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
select max(pfn.pathfullname) keep (dense_rank last order by hv.versionobjid) pathfullname
from harversions hv, harpathfullname pfn
where pfn.versionobjid=hv.versionobjid
group by hv.itemobjid
}}}
}}}

Would be translated to:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
select (array_agg(pfn.pathfullname order by hv.versionobjid desc nulls first,
                   pfn.pathfullname desc nulls last))[1] pathfullname
from harversions hv, harpathfullname pfn
where pfn.versionobjid=hv.versionobjid
group by hv.itemobjid
}}}
}}}

An example configuration:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
@parts/sqlrelay-querytranslations-keep-dense-rank-to-array-agg.conf@
}}}
}}}

The module has no module-specific attributes - only the standard '''module''', '''enabled''', and '''debug''' attributes apply.

Normalizing the query first helps the module identify //keep (dense_rank ...)// calls more reliably, as their syntactic form (the spacing around //keep//, //dense_rank//, //order by//, etc.) is sensitive to whitespace and case.

Note that in this example, the normalize query translation is loaded prior to the keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg query translation.  Pattern matching is substantially more reliable and efficient if the query has been normalized first.  See the [normalize.html#normalize normalize] query translation for more information.

== Considerations ==

The module performs the following transformations on each //max(EXPR) keep (dense_rank first|last order by SORT)// or //min(...)// call, where SORT may include an optional //asc// or //desc// direction and an optional //nulls first// or //nulls last// clause:

* wraps the call in an ordered //array_agg(EXPR order by ...)// and takes its first element
* for //dense_rank last//, reverses the sort direction and nulls placement, since only the first element of the array can be taken
* adds EXPR itself as a secondary sort key (descending for //max()//, ascending for //min()//, nulls last), so that ties in SORT resolve to a deterministic value

Calls inside string literals are left alone.  Identifiers like //mymax(...)// that merely end in //max// or //min// are also left alone.

Only a single sort key is supported inside //order by//, and only a single argument is supported inside //max()// or //min()//; either case is passed through untranslated.  An analytic form, eg. //max(x) keep (dense_rank first order by y) over (...)//, also can't be expressed this way and is left untranslated.

EXPR appears twice in the rewritten query.  For a plain column reference this makes no difference, but a volatile EXPR (eg. a call to a random-number function) would be evaluated twice by the rewritten query where Oracle's //keep()// evaluates it once.

